WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.164
Welcome to HarnessStation.

2
00:00:01.164 --> 00:00:06.000
Before we install anything, let's be clear about what this app actually is — and what it deliberately isn't.

3
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.400
Here's the one-line version, straight from the project's own README: it's a desktop app for running AI models as agents.

4
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.000
Not another chat box — a harness that lets a model do real work on your machine.

5
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.941
One thing to get straight early: HarnessStation does not include a model.

6
00:00:17.941 --> 00:00:18.707
You bring your own.

7
00:00:18.707 --> 00:00:23.744
That can be a free local model through Ollama or LM Studio, or a cloud model like OpenAI or Anthropic using your own API key.

8
00:00:23.744 --> 00:00:26.000
The app is the harness around whatever model you choose.

9
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:35.539
What the app supplies is everything the model needs to be useful: a place to hold conversations, tools to act with, your files and knowledge, long-term memory, a real browser it can drive, a voice to talk to, and automation to run things on a schedule.

10
00:00:35.539 --> 00:00:38.000
That's the whole point of the series — we'll cover each of these.

11
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.168
And just as important — what it deliberately doesn't do.

12
00:00:40.168 --> 00:00:41.406
No API keys ship inside the app.

13
00:00:41.406 --> 00:00:44.310
There's no account to create, no telemetry phoning home, and no cloud sync.

14
00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:48.219
Your keys live in your operating system's credential store, and your conversations stay on your disk.

15
00:00:48.219 --> 00:00:50.000
That's a design choice, not a missing feature.

16
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.035
Think of this as the map for the whole course.

17
00:00:52.035 --> 00:01:00.000
Everything you see here is a module we'll work through — starting simple with a first conversation, and building up to local models, agents, automation and the developer internals.

18
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.129
One honest note before we go on.

19
00:01:01.129 --> 00:01:04.588
This is early software, and the project says so itself — the docs call out rough edges on purpose.

20
00:01:04.588 --> 00:01:06.565
You'll hit the occasional sharp corner, and that's fine.

21
00:01:06.565 --> 00:01:09.000
I'll point them out as we go instead of pretending they aren't there.

22
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.175
That's the what and the why.

23
00:01:10.175 --> 00:01:14.413
Next episode, we install it on Windows — including the security warning every viewer is going to see.

24
00:01:14.413 --> 00:01:15.000
See you there.

